'''''Cistus laurifolius''''', commonly called '''laurel-leaf cistus''',<ref>{{GRIN | accessdate = 16 January 2018}}</ref> '''laurel-leaved cistus'''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=2014-10-17 }}</ref> or '''laurel-leaved rock rose''', is a [[species]] of highly branched flowering [[evergreen]] [[shrub]] [[native plant|native]] to some areas around the [[Mediterranean Sea|Mediterranean]].

==Description==
It grows {{convert|0.8|-|2|m|abbr=on}} high. The branches are strong and erect, with reddish bark that is easily removed in strips. The leaves are larger than in the other species of ''Cistus'', up to {{convert|9|cm|0|abbr=on}} long, [[leaf shape|lanceolate]], dark green, while the underside is whitish due to [[trichome]]s. The flowering occurs in late spring (May–June), later than most rockroses.<ref>{{cite book |last=Filippi |first=Olivier |date=2007 |title=Pour un jardin sans arrosage (For a garden without irrigation) |location=Arles |publisher=Actes Sud |page=207 |isbn=978-2-7427-6730-4}}</ref> It bears white flowers with a yellow spot at the base of each petal, of 4.5–5&nbsp;cm diameter<ref name="Iberica">{{cite journal |title=Cistus laurifolius|journal=[[Flora Iberica]] |volume=3 |page=331 |url=http://www.floraiberica.es/floraiberica/texto/pdfs/03_066_01_Cistus.pdf |access-date=21 March 2022}}</ref> It is widely cultivated in gardens, and has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>http://apps.rhs.org.uk/plantselector/plant?plantid=430 {{Dead link|date=February 2022}}</ref>

==Distribution and habitat ==
''Cistus laurifolius'' has a [[disjunct distribution|disjunct natural distribution]] throughout the [[Mediterranean Basin]], being found west in Morocco, [[Iberia]] (Portugal, Spain), avoiding the wetter climate in the northwest, southern France, Corsica and Tuscany (Italy), and east in Greece and [[Anatolia]].<ref name="Iberica"/>

With the general warming of the atmosphere and the consequent withdrawal of glacial ice, flora surviving from [[Tertiary period|Tertiary]] times could not re-establish their range in southern Europe;{{clarify|date=June 2012}} the new post-glacial climate was drier than that of the Tertiary. The original tropical European flora evolved into the present Mediterranean [[sclerophyll]] flora.<ref>{{cite journal|title=Mediterranean Garigue and Macchia (first page)|author=John W. Harshberger|journal=Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society|volume=65|issue=1|year=1926|pages=56–63|jstor=984338}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|author1=Fernández-Mazuecos, M. |author2=Vargas, P. |year=2010|title=Ecological rather than geographical isolation dominates Quaternary formation of Mediterranean ''Cistus'' species|journal=Molecular Ecology|volume=19|issue=7|pages=1381–1395|doi=10.1111/j.1365-294x.2010.04549.x|pmid=20196815|bibcode=2010MolEc..19.1381F |s2cid=19450012 }}</ref><ref name="rjb.csic.es">{{cite journal|journal=Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution|volume=37|issue=3|year=2005|pages=644–660|url=http://www.rjb.csic.es/jardinbotanico/ficheros/documentos/pdf/pubinv/PVG/MPE_37%282%29_644-660.pdf|title=Systematics, character evolution, and biogeography of ''Cistus'' L. (Cistaceae) based on ITS, trnL-trnF, and matK sequences|author1=Beatriz Guzmán |author2=Pablo Vargas |doi=10.1016/j.ympev.2005.04.026|pmid=16055353|bibcode=2005MolPE..37..644G }}</ref> The distribution of some surviving species, such as ''Cistus laurifolius'', shifted to wetter areas, such as the mountains.<ref name="rjb.csic.es"/> Due to this, ''C.&nbsp;laurifolius'' is named in Spanish in its distribution area as "mountain rockrose",<ref name="rjb.csic.es"/> although in the moister coastal west and northwest Iberian Peninsula, it is found at sea level.<ref>{{cite journal|title=Aportaciones A La Flora De Galicia, I.|author1=Antonio Rigueiro Rodríguez |author2=Fco. Javier Silva-Pando |url=http://www.rjb.csic.es/jardinbotanico/ficheros/documentos/pdf/anales/1983/Anales_40%282%29_385_395.pdf|journal=[[Anales Jard. Bot. Madrid]]|volume=40|issue=2|pages=385–395}}</ref>{{dubious|reason=found in Galicia is not the same as found at sea level|date=June 2012}}

''Cistus'' shrubland, including ''C.&nbsp;laurifolius'', resprouts after fire and has seeds that germinate after fires.<ref>{{cite journal|url=http://frames.nacse.org/ttrs/12000/12500.html|title=Space-time heterogeneity in the recovery after experimental burning and cutting in a ''Cistus laurifolius'' shrubland|author1=R. Tárrega|author2=E. Luis-Calabuig|author3=I. Alonso|journal=Plant Ecology|volume=129|issue=2|pages=179–187|year=1997|doi=10.1023/A:1009728317705|bibcode=1997PlEco.129..179T |s2cid=1870517|access-date=2012-05-10|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120709171514/http://frames.nacse.org/ttrs/12000/12500.html|archive-date=2012-07-09|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

==Phylogeny==
